{"id": "2ae0b706-911d-43fa-877e-d8b311a0dda4", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T10:21:48.255211", "source": "user", "parent_id": "0fc2d817-2888-4fc4-b2de-f26e14ff6582", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{"researcher_request": "Хорошо, план принимается — подход согласован, иди дальше. But I'm approving with conditions, не с пустыми руками. Your inspection is clean and I believe it: one file in /workspace, no games, no weights, no torch, no DNS. So the deliverable is a protocol that would run somewhere else, and nothing in it may read as if it ran.\n\nWhat I agree with, so you don't re-litigate it:\n- The two-phase decomposition (SFT on GPT-4 demos → self-play) is the right spine. You're correct that a game swap alone answers nothing.\n- A0 (untreated base anchor) and A1 (SFT-only, no self-play) are mandatory, not optional. Without A1 the A2-vs-A3/A4 result is permanently confounded with \"Taboo demos are just better text\", and that is the single most likely way this whole design fails.\n- Your answer to (a) — each arm gets its own paired, game-matched, token-matched SFT phase — is the right default. Keep it, and keep it labelled as a design choice, not a paper fact, since the brief genuinely does not pin it down.\n- Your answer to (b) — match compute (SFT tokens, self-play episodes, gradient steps), leave dialogue structure native — also agreed. Forcing 20Q into Taboo turn counts would kill the manipulation.\n\nNow the requirements for the full design. These are the gaps I want closed:\n\n1) Game-difficulty confound. Taboo, 20Q and Guess-My-City are not equally hard, and an easier game can produce a better-looking learning curve while teaching less. So specify how you normalize in-game signal: per-arm win-rate against a fixed frozen opponent/judge held out of training, plus a \"did the game actually get learned\" check (plateau before eval, not a dead-flat or still-climbing curve). State the pre-registered rule for calling a game \"trained enough to be a fair comparison\".\n\n2) Demo-quality control, not just demo-count control. Token-matched SFT is not enough if Taboo demos are longer/more varied chains of reasoning than 20Q demos. Add a reported descriptor set for each demo corpus (mean and total tokens, turns per episode, and a coarse measure of reasoning-chain length per turn) so a reader can see whether arms differ in data quality before self-play even starts. Report, don't optimize.\n\n3) A decoy/active control arm. Add one SFT-only arm on general instruction-style text matched in token count, sitting alongside A1. That tells us how much of any gain is \"kept training a model on chat-shaped text\" rather than the game. Name it clearly and keep it cheap.\n\n4) Primary outcome must be pinned before anything else. One headline number, one comparison, pre-registered: (post − base) normalized gain on the reasoning suite, A2 vs the A3/A4 average, paired by seed. Everything else is secondary and labelled as such. Also fix the answer-normalization/extraction spec, since GSM8K-style scoring is mostly regex luck otherwise. The benchmark list itself you've flagged as unconfirmed — keep it as a named parameter with your recollection marked unverified, do not present it as source fact.\n\n5) Seed count honesty. 3 seeds per arm, 5–6 arms is a small n for a between-arm difference claim. State plainly what is and isn't claimable at that n, and specify the paired-across-seeds statistic and what you do if one seed diverges (pre-registered rule, not \"we'll look at it\").\n\n6) Compute and feasibility section, concretely. Give a defensible estimate in GPU-hours per arm for SFT and self-play separately, plus the GPT-4 demo-generation dependency as an external input that cannot be satisfied here (network disabled, no checkpoints). End with an explicit \"what could be validated in this environment\" list — schema/config dry-runs, metric-scoring logic on hand-written placeholder inputs — and a matching \"what cannot\" list. No fabricated runs, numbers or curves.\n\nOne last thing: where the brief is silent, write \"unknown\" or \"design choice\", never a confident-sounding reconstruction. Your Llama-2-7B-chat and benchmark recollections are fine as flagged guesses; if they drift into the design as facts I'll send it back. Deliver the full protocol, per-arm configs, analysis plan and feasibility write-up.", "required_handoff_stage": "result", "approach_approved": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "61704144-08ab-4239-9737-e3b5da2176d6", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T10:22:35.893407", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "2ae0b706-911d-43fa-877e-d8b311a0dda4", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "The approved plan is clear. Before writing the final protocol, let me re-verify the two facts the feasibility section will lean on most (no torch/GPU/network), so nothing in the design overstates what this environment supports."}], "reasoning_content": "Here, I need to present the complete design at stage=result. Cover all six requirements: game difficulty confounding + training-sufficient rules, descriptor set for demo quality, decoy control arm (name: A6? Let's name the arms clearly), fixed primary outcome + normalization spec, honesty about seed count + pre-registered paired statistics + divergence rules, compute estimate + what can/cannot be verified in this environment.

Let's build the arm list:
- A0 — base model, no training (anchor).
- A1 — SFT only, on Adversarial Taboo GPT-4 demos.
- A2 — SPAG: Taboo SFT → adversarial self-play.
- A3 — 20-Question SFT → 20Q self-play.
- A4 — Guess-My-City SFT → GMC self-play.
- A5 — decoy: SFT only, on general instruction text matched in token count (researcher requested "an SFT-only arm on token-count-matched general instruction-style text, placed alongside A1"). Name: A5-Instruct-SFT. Wait, previously I proposed A5 as self-play without SFT (optional). The researcher added the decoy requirement. Let's reassign: A5 = decoy SFT on general instructions (mandatory per researcher). A6 (optional) = self-play on Taboo without SFT — keep as optional. Then arm count is 6 (A0–A5) + optional A6. 5–6 arms; matches researcher's "5–6 arms." Should A6 be dropped to keep it lean? Keep it optional and mention the budget impact.

Pre-registration of primary outcome: Δ_norm(arm) = mean across benchmarks b of (post_acc − base_acc)/(1 − base_acc)? Normalized gain options: simple diff, normalized gain (diff / (1 − base)) ("Bennett's normalized gain"). Headline number: mean normalized gain across benchmarks per seed. Comparison: A2 vs mean of A3 and A4, paired by seed. At n=3 seeds, a paired t-test is underpowered; pre-register: report mean differences with seed-level scatter, plus a permutation test of paired differences across seeds (2^3=8 sign-flip permutations → minimum achievable p is 0.25 at n=3, worth stating honestly). In fact, with 3 paired seeds and a sign-flip permutation test on the mean paired difference, minimum two-sided p = 2/8 = 0.25. So honest statement: inferential claims at seed n=3 are essentially impossible; seeds treat seed-to-seed variability, not population variance; benchmark items provide the main source of variance — paired item-level bootstrap/ McNemar within each seed's checkpoint comparison (A2 vs A3, A2 vs A4 each seed). Better: the primary claim is "difference in gain magnitude, descriptively, with item-level bootstrap CIs"; state explicitly what cannot be claimed. Pre-registered decision rule: declare superiority only if the mean paired difference is positive across all 3 seeds and the item-level bootstrap 95% CI for the gain difference on each seed excludes 0? Careful — design honestly: at n=3 seeds, we can claim that seed-level consistency is directional only; primary inference is item-level (thousands of questions per benchmark) but the unit of replication is the checkpoint, so CIs on model-level differences are optimistic. State plainly: at 3 seeds we can claim at best "consistent directional effect across 3 seeds"; a population-level superiority claim would require ≥7–10 seeds or hierarchical modeling; pre-register the rule: declare superiority only if mean Δ(A2) > mean Δ(A3,A4) in all 3 seeds and the effect on the pooled suite is above a minimum effect thre